## 9. Console Visual Design & Safety — the 2026-07-19 Design Session

> Six more forks decided the same day as the live shakedown, over the same channel-marker grammar: the
> work-flavor's visual identity, the transcript→bus streams architecture and the self-injection safety
> boundary it must hold, the avatar's two-publisher arbitration, the harness monitor's perceive/act split,
> and the AUQ question widget's native-vs-relay fork.

### [CXC-ICD-D17] Console work-flavor visual design = collaboration LAYOUT + instrument PALETTE (the "blend")

- decision: what layout+palette combination does the work-flavor console use — an "instrument-cockpit" full treatment (layout and palette both instrument-styled), a "collaboration-surface" full treatment (layout and palette both collaboration-styled), or a deliberate blend across the two axes?
- chosen: blend — collaboration LAYOUT (chat-forward hero, calm ambient rail, prominent self-avatar) wearing instrument PALETTE (dark ground ~#070a0f, one azure accent #4ea8ff, semantic ok/warn/critical kept separate from the accent, tabular-mono telemetry), with the instrument context gauge (65/80/90 threshold zones) and the `self/*` LEDGER + `self/registered` checklist in the rail. Only the context gauge is allowed to "warm".
- alternatives: (a) instrument-cockpit (both layout and palette instrument-styled — dense, telemetry-forward throughout); (b) collaboration-surface (both layout and palette collaboration-styled — warm, chat-app-like throughout).
- rationale: the calm, chat-forward collaboration layout keeps the human conversation primary — this is a co-equal peer, not a cockpit to operate — while the instrument palette's discipline (one accent, separated semantics, tabular-mono) keeps telemetry legible and prevents the whole surface from "warming" indiscriminately; only the thing that should alarm (context) is allowed to.
- invalidating_assumption: none material — a preference call among two real, built pre-vis directions (docs/intelligence-console/previs/console-blended.html).
- recoverability: PREFERENCE (David's aesthetic call among real alternatives)
- status: ratified
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-DECISIONS]

### [CXC-ICD-D18] Transcript→bus streams: a standalone tailer republishes distinct semantic namespaces

- decision: does the console surface what the Intelligence SAYS and DOES by driving all UI state off the live channel only, or by adding a standalone read-only tailer over the JSONL transcript that republishes distinct semantic namespaces?
- chosen: a standalone read-only tailer (BUILT: intelligence/publisher/transcript_publisher.py) re-publishes the transcript as three semantic namespaces, each bound by its own sub-widget: thread/narration ← assistant TEXT blocks; self/activity ← tool_use/tool_result reduced to {tool,target,phase,dur_s}; self/subagents ← Task/Agent lifecycle reduced to {type,model,desc,status,tokens,dur_s}.
- alternatives: drive all of these off the live channel only, with no transcript tailer.
- rationale: the JSONL transcript is already the authoritative record of what the Intelligence says and does; a passive tailer decouples the console's state-on-bus from the channel's liveness and gives each concern (narration, tool activity, subagent lifecycle) its own namespace and widget — Problem 4 (state on bus, behavior in widget) made concrete, and [CXC-IC-CANVAS]'s composable-canvas principle extended from one diagram namespace to a full activity surface.
- invalidating_assumption: the JSONL transcript format stays stable enough to tail; if Claude Code's transcript schema changes materially, the tailer's parsing must be re-grounded.
- recoverability: DERIVABLE
- status: resolved
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-DECISIONS]

### [CXC-ICD-D19] Narration never rides the actuation channel — self-injection ruled out by construction

- decision: does narration (thread/narration, mirroring assistant TEXT blocks) ride the same channel namespace as actuation (intelligence/<id>/out), or does it get its own namespace?
- chosen: narration rides its own thread/narration namespace, strictly separate from the actuation channel intelligence/<id>/out; the tailer also enforces a hard boundary — thinking blocks are dropped (never mirrored) and tool_result CONTENT is never republished (only tool/target/phase/dur_s metadata).
- alternatives: republish narration onto (or alongside) the actuation channel intelligence/<id>/out.
- rationale: a narration text block that happens to look like a JSON command blob would, on the actuation channel, be parsed and actuated by the launcher — a self-injection loop where the Intelligence's own thinking-aloud text could trigger unintended actuation. Routing narration through a namespace the launcher-actuator never parses makes that impossible BY CONSTRUCTION, not by validation. The boundary also encodes what the console is FOR: mirror what the Intelligence SAYS and DOES, never what it THINKS (thinking blocks) or the tool plumbing's GUTS (tool_result content).
- invalidating_assumption: none material for the current construction; if a future feature needs the launcher to parse narration for directives, the actuation-channel separation must be preserved by adding authentication (see follow-up), not by merging namespaces.
- follow-up: OPEN — the launcher's actuation parser should AUTHENTICATE directives on intelligence/<id>/out (defense-in-depth) so the actuation channel itself cannot be spoofed by another bus publisher; not yet built.
- recoverability: DERIVABLE
- status: resolved
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-DECISIONS]

### [CXC-ICD-D20] Two-publisher avatar model, arbitrated by priority

- decision: does self/avatar/expression have exactly one publisher (the Intelligence itself), or two — the Intelligence explicit + a monitor-inferred fallback — and if two, how is the conflict arbitrated?
- chosen: two publishers — the Intelligence itself (explicit, an act of self-representation) and a monitor that INFERS an expression from JSONL activity when the Intelligence doesn't push one; a PriorityCommandSource arbitrates so the explicit expression ALWAYS wins over the inferred, and the inferred only fills when the Intelligence is heads-down (so the face is never blank).
- alternatives: a single publisher — the Intelligence's explicit self/avatar/expression only, with the avatar going idle/blank when the Intelligence hasn't pushed one recently.
- rationale: Problem 4b (control is a bus peer) applied to a shared namespace with two legitimate writers: the explicit publish is the Intelligence's own act of self-representation and must win when present; but heads-down stretches (tool-heavy work with no self-narration) would otherwise leave the avatar blank, which reads as broken rather than busy. The inferred fallback, subordinate via priority arbitration, keeps the avatar alive without ever overriding the Intelligence's own voice.
- invalidating_assumption: none material — architectural resolution of a genuine authority conflict.
- recoverability: CONTESTED (architect wanted to drive the avatar directly from the Intelligence; David wanted it always animated — the priority-arbitrated fallback reconciles both)
- status: resolved
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-DECISIONS]

### [CXC-ICD-D21] Harness monitor: perceive/act split (statusline perceives, launcher acts)

- decision: does the harness monitor (context-threshold notifications, quiescence heartbeat, on-demand context query) combine perception (reading Claude Code's context state) and action (deciding when to notify) in one component, or split them?
- chosen: split — perception is owned by the statusline command (the only node handed Claude Code's authoritative used_percentage; it writes a state file the launcher reads, PRIMARY source, JSONL estimate as FALLBACK, always source-labeled); action (crossing-state fire-once+re-arm at 65/80/90, quiescence detection, delivery) is owned by the long-running launcher (BUILT: intelligence/launcher/monitor.py); delivery rides intelligence/<id>/in as NON-interrupting <channel from="harness"> events, distinct from the ESC-ESC PTY inject reserved for /compact.
- alternatives: a single component both reads context state and decides/delivers notifications (no perceive/act split); or the launcher re-estimates context itself from JSONL without the statusline's authoritative figure.
- rationale: Problem 1 (perceive/act split) — Claude Code hands the authoritative used_percentage ONLY to the statusline command, not to an external monitor; a monitor that tried to estimate context itself would duplicate [CXC-IC-SELF]'s stdin-primary/JSONL-fallback precedence problem instead of reusing it. Splitting perception (statusline, source-labeled state file) from action (launcher, fire-once+re-arm state machine) keeps each piece single-purpose and reuses the same authority precedence [CXC-IC-SELF] already established. Non-interrupting delivery (harness channel event, not a PTY keystroke) keeps the 65/80/90 notices from competing with the Intelligence's own /compact authoring at [CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE].
- invalidating_assumption: Claude Code continues to expose used_percentage only via the statusline hook; if a direct API exposes it to external processes, the statusline relay becomes optional (the perceive/act split remains, the source narrows).
- recoverability: DERIVABLE
- status: resolved-pending-live-confirmation (query + heartbeat glass-green live 2026-07-19; the 65/80/90 thresholds are machine-green (9/9) pending their live-fire condition)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-DECISIONS]

### [CXC-ICD-D22] AUQ question widget: ComposeIO-native emit, not channel-relay

- decision: does the console's structured-choice widget depend on Claude Code exposing AskUserQuestion (AUQ) over the channel contract (channel-relay), or does the Intelligence emit its own ComposeIO-native question namespace independent of whether AUQ crosses the channel?
- chosen: ComposeIO-native — the Intelligence publishes to intelligence/<id>/question/request (or thread/question/request) carrying the AUQ schema (questions, headers, options, multiSelect, previews); a widget renders interactive option cards; the human's choice publishes to .../question/verdict; the launcher-actuator/channel delivers it back to the Intelligence as its AUQ answer.
- alternatives: wait on / depend on Claude Code exposing AskUserQuestion over the channels contract directly (channel-relay), mirroring the permission-relay pattern of [CXC-IC-PERMISSION].
- rationale: AUQ is currently terminal-only and does not cross the channel, so structured A/B/N-option decisions degrade to free-text prose over the existing channel — a real loss for exactly the moments (forks) worth capturing. A native emit works on ANY bus regardless of whether/when Anthropic exposes AUQ over channels, generalizes the permission-relay pattern from binary (allow/deny) to N structured options, and closes a loop the decision-extractor already assumes: it already treats AUQ forks as the decision-ledger source, so a native AUQ widget makes the console the place decisions are BOTH made (widget) and captured (extractor) — this ledger's own methodology, made structural.
- invalidating_assumption: Claude Code exposes AskUserQuestion over the channels contract in a future release — then the native emit and the channel-native path can run in parallel, or the native path can defer to the platform one; the widget/namespace contract stays valid either way.
- recoverability: CONTESTED (native-emit vs channel-relay — chose native)
- status: resolved
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-DECISIONS]

